WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:23.250 --> 00:00:28.840
You may have never heard of German chemist
Fritz Haber, but there is a 50% chance that

00:00:28.840 --> 00:00:32.910
you owe your own existence to this man’s
invention.

00:00:32.910 --> 00:00:37.730
The process he designed to convert atmospheric
nitrogen into ammonia made it possible to

00:00:37.730 --> 00:00:43.900
produce fertilizers on a large scale, ensuring
much more plentiful harvests than ever before

00:00:43.900 --> 00:00:48.550
in the history of mankind and therefore saving
billions of people from starvation.

00:00:48.550 --> 00:00:54.550
It is estimated that the food base of half
of the current World population is based on

00:00:54.550 --> 00:00:56.690
his Haber-Bosch process.

00:00:56.690 --> 00:01:01.140
This achievement granted Dr Haber the highest
of accolades, the Nobel Prize for chemistry,

00:01:01.140 --> 00:01:02.140
in 1918.

00:01:02.140 --> 00:01:06.650
And yet, there is a darker side to this benefactor
of humanity.

00:01:06.650 --> 00:01:13.400
A story of family abuse, suicide, blind loyalty
taken to the extreme and the first ever use

00:01:13.400 --> 00:01:16.060
of weapons of mass destruction on a battlefield.

00:01:16.060 --> 00:01:17.070
This is the story of how Fritz Haber, the
man who ended famine, became the father of

00:01:17.070 --> 00:01:18.070
chemical warfare.

00:01:18.070 --> 00:01:21.270
This is the story of how Fritz Haber, the
Jewish scientist, unwittingly created the

00:01:21.270 --> 00:01:31.460
ultimate weapon that brought about the Holocaust.

00:01:31.460 --> 00:01:35.780
The life of Fritz Haber has been described
as worthy of a Greek tragic hero, featuring

00:01:35.780 --> 00:01:42.020
from its very first years all the ingredients
of a Classic tragedy: father and son relationship

00:01:42.020 --> 00:01:44.780
conflict, the early shadow of death, hubris.

00:01:44.780 --> 00:01:47.120
And explosives.

00:01:47.120 --> 00:01:53.300
Fritz Haber was born on December the 9th,
1868, in Breslau, Prussia, now Wroclaw in

00:01:53.300 --> 00:01:54.300
Poland.

00:01:54.300 --> 00:01:58.100
His parents, Siegfried and Paula, were both
members of a wealthy Jewish family, well established

00:01:58.100 --> 00:01:59.549
in the area since the early 1800s.

00:01:59.549 --> 00:02:04.799
To be more precise, they were members of the
same family: they were in fact first cousins

00:02:04.799 --> 00:02:07.290
who had married against the will of their
relatives.

00:02:07.290 --> 00:02:11.820
Haber’s life had barely started when it
was already struck by tragedy: his mother

00:02:11.820 --> 00:02:15.160
died from complications only three weeks after
childbirth.

00:02:15.160 --> 00:02:19.100
His father Siegfried was devastated, leaving
Fritz in the care of relatives.

00:02:19.100 --> 00:02:24.590
As a consequence, the two grew cold and distant,
and their relationship never recovered.

00:02:24.590 --> 00:02:29.500
When his son was six, Siegfried remarried
and had three daughters, Else, Helene and

00:02:29.500 --> 00:02:32.690
Frieda, with whom Fritz actually got along
well.

00:02:32.690 --> 00:02:38.900
But the life of Fritz was to be shaped by
his own ambitions, rather than his family’s

00:02:38.900 --> 00:02:43.560
influence: the ambition to master science
from an early age and the ambition to prove

00:02:43.560 --> 00:02:50.120
that he was first a German, then a Jew, ready
to serve his Kaiser and his recently unified

00:02:50.120 --> 00:02:51.140
fatherland.

00:02:51.140 --> 00:02:54.810
After completing high school in 1886, Fritz
declined an offer to become an apprentice

00:02:54.810 --> 00:02:56.160
at his father’s dye company.

00:02:56.160 --> 00:03:00.250
The two men frequently clashed and simply
could not work together.

00:03:00.250 --> 00:03:04.180
Instead, Fritz applied to study chemistry
in Berlin, earning his doctorate cum laude

00:03:04.180 --> 00:03:05.940
in 1891.

00:03:05.940 --> 00:03:10.360
Having achieved a doctorate in less than five
years was already impressive enough – moreover,

00:03:10.360 --> 00:03:14.200
Fritz had to interrupt his studies for one
year of compulsory military service in an

00:03:14.200 --> 00:03:15.560
artillery regiment.

00:03:15.560 --> 00:03:45.489
This was to be his first experience with the
explosive power

00:03:45.489 --> 00:22:55.100
of modern warfare.

